    "<br>\n",
    "\n",
    "# Enough about lists, let's talk this *tuple* thing\n",
    "\n",
    "<center><img src=\"https://i.postimg.cc/CMBqG340/sequences-tuples.jpg\" width=500></center>\n",
    "\n",
    "<br>\n",
    "\n",
    "### Tuples (at first glance) are VERY similar to lists\n",
    "\n",
    "```python\n",
    "    MyTuple = ( 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 )\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "* They contain comma-separated values\n",
    "* They use parentheses `( )` instead of square brackets `[ ]`\n",
    "* They can even be made from `,` separated values\n",
    "* Can be pronounced *tuh-pull* or *too-pull*"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "f824570e-aa83-49ab-8923-1d9db1965d92",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "<class 'tuple'>\n",
      "(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "MyTuple = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)  # note the parentheses instead of square brackets\n",
    "print( type(MyTuple) )\n",
    "print( MyTuple )\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "57431700-3d7d-4005-84a1-01d89befd545",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### But they really only need the commas\n",
    "> * NOTE: It is the **comma** that makes a tuple, **NOT** the parentheses"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "77714bb1-2fca-4cc3-9dfb-d95ea281275f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "<class 'tuple'>\n",
      "(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "MyTuple = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5  # note no parenthese, but still a tuple\n",
    "print( type(MyTuple) )\n",
    "print( MyTuple )"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d0655b80-a1d0-418b-a969-1cd1fe478d3f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<br>\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Similar to lists, tuples can contain different data types"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "60076a53-197b-445d-9649-7b4a8252cbe2",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "('red', 'blue', 'green', 12345)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "spam = ( \"red\", \"blue\", \"green\", 12345 )\n",
    "print( spam )"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "eab2a525-a667-4549-ace8-06fd5b483d9f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<br>\n",
    "\n",
    "### Slicing Tuples and a **point of confusion!!!**\n",
    "* Even though we create a tuple using parentheses instead of square brackets\n",
    "* Indexing and slicing is **ALWAYS** performed with square brackets `[]` (regardless of data type)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "id": "262bae64-2d13-4490-8382-ea82219715b9",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "index 2 blue\n",
      "index 1 to the end ('blue', 'green', 12345)\n",
      "index 1 to the end ('blue', 'green', 12345)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "print( \"index 2\", spam[1] )\n",
    "print( \"index 1 to the end\", spam[1:5] )\n",
    "print( \"index 1 to the end\", spam[1:5] )"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "908c18e7-9161-4c80-8cf9-f4fdde98a1e2",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<br>\n",
    "\n",
    "### Lists vs Tuples: So what's the difference?\n",
    "\n",
    "<center>\n",
    "<figure>\n",
    "<img src=\"https://i.postimg.cc/CMC7nv7Y/06-mutable-immutable.png\" alt=\"Mutable vs. Immutable\" width=400>\n",
    "<figcaption>Mutable vs. Immutable <a href=\"https://algodaily.com/lessons/mutability-vs-immutability\">source</a></figcaption>\n",
    "</figure>\n",
    "</center>\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "* **Mutable** vs. **Immutable** data types\n",
    "\n",
    "    - **Lists:** are mutable\n",
    "    - **Tuples and Strings:** are NOT mutable.  They are immutable"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "948df961-37b2-4173-a2f4-ae6d301ce37b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Can change a list"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "ac6ee48e-986d-4025-be26-36f01f175684",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "['red', 'green', 'blue', 'yellow']\n",
      "['red', 'black', 'blue', 'yellow']\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "ListColors = [ 'red', 'green', 'blue', 'yellow' ]\n",
    "\n",
    "# Changing colors in a list is simple #\n",
    "print( ListColors )\n",
    "\n",
    "ListColors[1] = 'black'\n",
    "\n",
    "print( ListColors )\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fe26fa9d-1334-4110-b518-48718374dabb",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<br>\n",
    "\n",
    "##### Cannot change a tuple"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "dfdfa41e-5c7f-498d-840e-d718fbabd912",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "('red', 'green', 'blue', 'yellow')\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "ename": "TypeError",
     "evalue": "'tuple' object does not support item assignment",
     "output_type": "error",
     "traceback": [
      "\u001b[31m---------------------------------------------------------------------------\u001b[39m",
      "\u001b[31mTypeError\u001b[39m                                 Traceback (most recent call last)",
      "\u001b[36mCell\u001b[39m\u001b[36m \u001b[39m\u001b[32mIn[5]\u001b[39m\u001b[32m, line 5\u001b[39m\n\u001b[32m      2\u001b[39m TupleColors = ( \u001b[33m'\u001b[39m\u001b[33mred\u001b[39m\u001b[33m'\u001b[39m, \u001b[33m'\u001b[39m\u001b[33mgreen\u001b[39m\u001b[33m'\u001b[39m, \u001b[33m'\u001b[39m\u001b[33mblue\u001b[39m\u001b[33m'\u001b[39m, \u001b[33m'\u001b[39m\u001b[33myellow\u001b[39m\u001b[33m'\u001b[39m )\n\u001b[32m      4\u001b[39m \u001b[38;5;28mprint\u001b[39m( TupleColors )\n\u001b[32m----> \u001b[39m\u001b[32m5\u001b[39m \u001b[43mTupleColors\u001b[49m\u001b[43m[\u001b[49m\u001b[32;43m1\u001b[39;49m\u001b[43m]\u001b[49m = \u001b[33m'\u001b[39m\u001b[33mblack\u001b[39m\u001b[33m'\u001b[39m   \u001b[38;5;66;03m# error\u001b[39;00m\n",
      "\u001b[31mTypeError\u001b[39m: 'tuple' object does not support item assignment"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Changing colors in a tuple.....not so much #\n",
    "TupleColors = ( 'red', 'green', 'blue', 'yellow' )\n",
    "\n",
    "print( TupleColors )\n",
    "TupleColors[1] = 'black'   # error\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "45dc6a73-6297-4a98-8ebb-1e325696fcb9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "<br>\n",
    "\n",
    "### In short, we can change the values in a list but **NOT** a tuple!\n",
    "\n",
    "<br>\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "#### So **why** would we ever use a tuple instead of a list?\n",
    "1. Let people reading your code know that you don't intend for the sequence to be changed\n",
    "    - Think menu items, etc.\n",
    "<br><br>\n",
    "2. Because the contents of a tuple never change, python can **optimize** them to make your code execute more efficiently\n",
    "    - Tuple is Python's <u>preferred</u> sequence in many cases\n",
    "    - Tuples are how multiple values are returned from functions\n",
    "    - Tuples are faster to use and use less memory resources\n",
    "    \n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "36d5f706-43df-4623-8919-79d2853e6e98",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "##### Example: function returns a tuple\n",
    "* We are going to return to **one** variable object only"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "id": "e36791aa-7a70-4093-bf87-24b06b369ecb",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import random\n",
    "\n",
    "def make_xyz( ):\n",
    "    x = random.randint(0,10)\n",
    "    y = random.randint(0,10)\n",
    "    z = random.randint(0,10)\n",
    "    return x, y, z  # return three values as a tuple"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
   "id": "cd89201e",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "<class 'tuple'>\n",
      "(9, 4, 5)\n",
      "x=9 y=4 z=5\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "\n",
    "point = make_xyz()   # note we return two values but gets stored in one\n",
    "\n",
    "print( type(point) )\n",
    "print( point )\n",
    "print( f\"x={point[0]} y={point[1]} z={point[2]}\" )"
   ]
  },
  {
